<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Daju kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1400 CE (approx.) · capital Jebel Marra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The earliest remembered kingdom of Darfur, centred on the Jebel Marra mountains of western Sudan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Daju kingdom is known mainly from oral tradition, a few Arabic references and archaeology, so its history is very uncertain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> traditions name the Daju as the first dynasty to rule the region, from around the 12th century, based in the fertile valleys and slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrah-mountains">Jebel Marra</a> massif.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Daju were a Nilo-Saharan-speaking people who farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a> and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, ruling over neighbouring peoples through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a>. Stone-built sites on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrah-mountains">Jebel Marra</a> may date from their era. The state probably took part in trade routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley and Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Traditions say the Daju were displaced by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tunjur-kingdom">Tunjur</a> around the 14th or 15th century, and Daju groups moved south and west, where Daju-speaking communities still live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kordofan">Kordofan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. The later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> looked back on them as its forerunners.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/daju-kingdom.jpg" alt="Daju kingdom" width="320" height="178"><figcaption>Image: Konrad Miller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Daju_kingdom,_al-Idrisi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jebel Marra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional African religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Daju</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Daju, Dajo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=daju-kingdom" aria-label="Daju kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5477 1571 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Daju kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5665 1903h0M5571 2116h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5797 1827h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5797 1827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Sudan. <a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=daju-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
